+# Cover archival
+
+Cover IDs are formatted as ten decimal digits. The first four digits identify
+an item of one million covers; the next two digits identify a batch of 10,000.
+For example, cover `0081234567` belongs to item `covers_0081` and batch `23`.
+The four sizes use separate items and ZIPs:
+
+| Size | Item | ZIP | Name inside ZIP |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Original | `covers_0081` | `covers_0081_23.zip` | `0081234567.jpg` |
+| Small | `s_covers_0081` | `s_covers_0081_23.zip` | `0081234567-S.jpg` |
+| Medium | `m_covers_0081` | `m_covers_0081_23.zip` | `0081234567-M.jpg` |
+| Large | `l_covers_0081` | `l_covers_0081_23.zip` | `0081234567-L.jpg` |
+
+ZIPs are stored under `data_root/items/<item>/<zip>`. They are uncompressed so
+archive.org can serve an individual image at
+`https://archive.org/download/<item>/<zip>/<name>`.
+
+`archive.archive(test=False)` stages one batch. It leaves database references
+pointing to local images. Missing files mark a cover as `failed` so it can be
+investigated. After staging, call
+`archive.Batch(item_id, batch_id).process_pending(upload=True, finalize=True)`.
+This checks that all four ZIPs are listed in their archive.org items and that
+every non-failed cover has an entry in each ZIP. Only then does it set
+`archived` and `uploaded`, replace the four filename fields with download URLs,
+and remove staged and local files. A per-batch file lock rejects simultaneous
+runs on the same range. Repeated ZIP writes skip names already present.
+
+Existing tar references remain readable through the legacy retrieval path;
+new batches use ZIPs.
